# Guide: add a new `db.Model`

Walks through adding a `Widget` model alongside the existing `Item`
model — follow this shape rather than inventing a new one. This covers
only the model + query half; for the full resource (schema, Blueprint,
route registration, test) see the `flask` skill's
`guides/add-a-resource.md`, which points back here for this step.

## 1. Define the model

In `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`, add the class next to `Item`:

```python
from sqlalchemy.orm import Mapped, mapped_column

from {{ package_name }}.core.db import db


class Item(db.Model):
    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)
    name: Mapped[str]
    description: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(default=None)


class Widget(db.Model):
    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)
    name: Mapped[str]
    description: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(default=None)
```

Always import `db` from `{{ package_name }}.core.db` — a model defined
against any other `SQLAlchemy()` instance won't show up in
`db.create_all()`{% if migrations %} or in `flask db migrate`'s autogenerate diff{% endif %},
and produces confusing "table not found" errors instead of an obvious
import error. Add columns with `Mapped[...]` + `mapped_column(...)`
(SQLAlchemy 2.0 declarative style, matching `Item`); a foreign key to
another model uses `db.relationship(...)` the same way plain
SQLAlchemy does — see the `sqlalchemy` skill for that syntax, it isn't
special here.

